otted line indicates the cut-off between the high-and the low-viral-load groups. The dashed lines indicate the median of each group.</p><p>Abbreviation: HBoV, human bocavirus.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjlm-31-179-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Distribution of viral load in HBoV-positive patients according to diseases.</p><p>Abbreviation: HBoV, human bocavirus.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjlm-31-179-g003"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Age distribution of human bocavirus-positive patients</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjlm-31-179-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>Human bocavirus positive patients were grouped into low-and high-viral-load groups using 1.0&#x000d7;10<sup>6</sup> copies/mL as the threshold value.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Comparison of demographic and clinical characteristics of patients hospitalized for human bocavirus infection on the basis of viral loads<sup>*</sup></p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjlm-31-179-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>Data presented as mean&#x000b1;standard deviation or number (%) of patients.</p><p>Abbreviations: WBC, white blood cell; ESR, erythrocyte sedimentation rate.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>